/* hunger.lps — Kowalski, ch. 8 §8.3: the time factor.
 *
 *     if I become hungry at a time
 *     then I have some food at a later time
 *     and I eat the food at the later time.
 *
 * and the causal belief that goes with it:
 *
 *     an animal has an object at a time
 *     if the animal is near the object at an earlier time
 *     and the animal picks up the object at the earlier time
 *     and nothing terminates the animal having the object between the two times.
 *
 * bookExamples.md marks this "not yet" for LE on two counts: the maintenance
 * goal, and the temporal-persistence belief with its "nothing terminates …
 * between the two times". In LPS the second one is not a belief you write at
 * all — persistence is what a fluent *is*, and the frame problem the sentence
 * is working around does not arise.
 */

maxTime(10).

fluents  hungry(_), near(_, _), has(_, _).
events   becomes_hungry(_), wanders_near(_, _).
actions  pick_up(_, _), eat(_, _).

initially near(me, apple).

becomes_hungry(A)     initiates hungry(A).
wanders_near(A, O)    initiates near(A, O).
pick_up(A, O)         initiates has(A, O).
eat(A, O)             terminates has(A, O).
eat(A, _)             terminates hungry(A).

food(apple).
food(bread).

%  The maintenance goal, with the book's temporal structure intact: the having
%  and the eating are at a *later* time than the hunger, and the engine is what
%  keeps them in that order.
if   hungry(A) at T1
then has(A, O) at T2, food(O), eat(A, O) from T2 to T3.

%  Getting to have something: near it, then pick it up. The persistence the
%  book has to state explicitly is free here.
if   hungry(A) at T1, near(A, O) at T1, food(O), not has(A, O) at T1
then pick_up(A, O) from T1 to T2.

false pick_up(A, O) from T1 to _, not near(A, O) at T1.

observe becomes_hungry(me) from 2 to 3.
